R='rudakov1974irreducible'
VGG='vershikgelfandgraev1975diffeomorphisms'
def ri(*args,**kw):
    kw.setdefault('subject','Infinite-dimensional representation theory')
    return item(310,*args,**kw)
talk(310,'1973-10-15','A. N. Rudakov','P000520-P000523','Discrete representations of formal vector fields',r'The dated talk is on P000521; the Kirillov intervention at the beginning of P000522 is kept with it and explicitly attributed. Rudakov introduces discrete continuous representations of formal vector fields, induction from finite jet quotients, and the minimal-height submodule. The high-height induction theorem and the differential-form exceptions match \citet[\S\S2--3,6,13]{rudakov1974irreducible}, received 9 October 1973 and published in 1974. Kirillov then suggests a diffeomorphism-group analogue, explicitly labelled unproved in the notebook. This is not evidence of a complete classification of arbitrary representations of the full diffeomorphism group.')
ri('P000521','definition','The formal vector-field algebra and its filtration',r'\(W_n=\sum_i k[[x_1,\ldots,x_n]]\partial_{x_i}\), with continuous inserted before derivations; \(\mathcal L_j\) is defined by degrees at least \(j+1\).',r'Over a field k of characteristic zero, let \(\mathfrak m=(x_1,\ldots,x_n)\) and \(W_n=\operatorname{Der}_{k,\rm cont} k[[x_1,\ldots,x_n]]\). For \(j\ge-1\), \(\mathcal L_j=\sum_i\mathfrak m^{j+1}\partial_{x_i}\). The order-of-vanishing filtration defines the linear topology; it is not the highest polynomial degree of a formal series.',r'\citet[\S1, pp.~836--838]{rudakov1974irreducible}.',[R],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction=r'Characteristic zero and the meaning of degree for formal power series are supplied.')
ri('P000521','definition','Continuity into a discrete countable-dimensional module',r'The notes write \(\rho:\mathcal L\to\operatorname{Aut}E\), and require a neighborhood V with \(\rho(v)x=0\).',r'A continuous Lie-algebra representation is a homomorphism \(\rho:\mathcal L\to\operatorname{End}_k(E)\) for a discrete, countable-dimensional vector space E such that every \(x\in E\) is killed by some \(\mathcal L_j\). Equivalently every orbit map is locally constant at zero.',r'\citet[Lemma~2.1, p.~839]{rudakov1974irreducible}.',[R],literal='false as written',reconstruction=r'Automorphisms is the wrong target for a Lie-algebra action: even the zero Lie element acts by the zero endomorphism, not an invertible map. The neighborhood quantifier means every v in V.')
ri('P000521','construction','Inflation from a finite jet quotient followed by induction',r'A representation of \(M/M^{\prime}\), with \(M^{\prime}\) of finite codimension, is inflated to M and induced to \(\mathcal L\); later M is \(\mathcal L_0\).',r'For an open Lie subalgebra \(\mathcal L_0\), a continuous module V that factors through \(\mathcal L_0/\mathcal L_r\) inflates to \(\mathcal L_0\) and gives \(U(\mathcal L)\otimes_{U(\mathcal L_0)}V\). Under the stated countable-dimensional discrete hypotheses the induced representation is continuous. A finite-dimensional quotient algebra does not require V itself to be finite-dimensional.',r'\citet[Definitions~2.2--2.3 and following observation, pp.~839--840]{rudakov1974irreducible}.',[R],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction=r'The abstract M in the opening programme is specialized to the open filtered subalgebra used in the theorem. No finite-dimensional-module hypothesis is added.')
ri('P000521','construction','Minimal height and the kernel submodule',r'\(p(x)=\min\{r:\mathcal L_rx=0\}\), \(\bar p(E)=\min_{x\ne0}p(x)\), and \(E_0=\{x:p(x)=\bar p(E)\}\) is asserted to be a submodule.',r'For nonzero x define p(x) by its annihilator in the filtration. Let h be the minimum over nonzero vectors. Then \(E_0=\{0\}\cup\{x\ne0:p(x)=h\}\) is a \(\mathcal L_0\)-submodule. Indeed p(x+y) is at most the maximum of the two heights, and \([\mathcal L_0,\mathcal L_h]\subseteq\mathcal L_h\).',r'\citet[Definition~3.1, Proposition~3.2 and Corollary~3.3, p.~840]{rudakov1974irreducible}.',[R],cls='A',literal='false as written',reconstruction=r'The printed paper explicitly adjoins zero; the notebook set omits it. The short argument here is a critical verification of the definition, not a new classification result.',development='annotated')
ri('P000521','theorem','Irreducibility and reconstruction above height one',r'If \(\bar p(E)>1\), the representation is induced: \(E=\operatorname{Ind}(E_0)\); the handwritten bound is retraced.',r'For a Cartan-type algebra \(\mathcal L\) in Rudakov\textquoteright s filtration, every irreducible discrete continuous module E of height greater than one has irreducible \(\mathcal L_0\)-kernel \(E_0\), and \(U(\mathcal L)\otimes_{U(\mathcal L_0)}E_0\to E\) is an isomorphism. Conversely induction of an irreducible \(\mathcal L_0\)-module of height greater than one is irreducible.',r'\citet[Theorem~6.1 and Corollaries~6.2--6.3, pp.~843--844]{rudakov1974irreducible}; the manuscript was received six days before this talk and appeared in 1974.',[R],cls='A',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction=r'The primary paper resolves the provisional bound as 1. Continuity, irreducibility, topology and filtration are inherited from the preceding definitions; the assertion is not about all abstract Wn-modules.')
ri('P000521','theorem','The differential-form exceptions for W_n',r'The opening says all irreducibles are induced with finitely many exceptions; the closing identifies \((d\Omega^p)^*\).',r'For \(W_n\), the exceptional height-one inducing \(\mathcal L_0\)-modules are the n modules inflated from the \(\mathfrak{gl}_n\)-modules with highest weights \((0,\ldots,0,-1,\ldots,-1)\), having p entries equal to \(-1\), \(1\le p\le n\). Each exceptional induced module has a unique maximal proper submodule; its irreducible quotient is the continuous dual of \(d\Omega^{p-1}\), the formal exact p-forms. Other irreducible height-one inducing modules give irreducible induced modules.',r'\citet[Definition~13.6, Theorem~13.7 and Corollaries~13.8--13.9, pp.~865--866]{rudakov1974irreducible}.',[R],cls='A',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction=r'The exact height-one statement is given instead of extending the shorthand to all unrestricted module categories. The dual is the continuous dual of the linearly compact formal-form space, hence discrete; the trivial representation is separately understood. Closed positive-degree formal forms are exact in characteristic zero.')
ri('P000521','claim','The corresponding Hamiltonian result',r'The last line says there are corresponding results for Hamiltonian ones as well.',r'For \(H_{2m}\), using its induced formal filtration, the same high-height induction and irreducibility statements hold. The notebook does not state a list of height-one Hamiltonian exceptions.',r'\citet[\S1 and Theorem~6.1, Corollaries~6.2--6.3]{rudakov1974irreducible} explicitly includes H alongside W, S and K.',[R],cls='A',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial',reconstruction=r'Only the precise high-height assertion supported by the paper is identified. The Wn exceptional list is not transferred to the Hamiltonian algebra.')
ri('P000521','question','The parenthetical bounded-representation question',r'\ru{\(\exists\) ли огр. непр. предст-я?} appears inside the discussion of height.',r'The abbreviation may ask about a bounded height, bounded operators under a norm, or another boundedness condition. No norm, common height bound or quantified class is specified.',r'\citet[\S\S2--3]{rudakov1974irreducible} defines continuity and height but does not determine the missing predicate of this parenthetical question.',[R],cls='F',literal='not determinable',match='thematic only',verification='unresolved lead',confidence='low',status='not sufficiently definite to receive a research-problem identifier.')
ri('P000522','question','Kirillov on finite-functional-dimension diffeomorphism representations',r'Kirillov asks for representations of \(\operatorname{Diff}M\) of finite functional dimension, induced from a finite-point stabilizer modulo a finite-jet stabilizer; the note says unproved but well known.',r'Given finitely many points of a smooth manifold, their stabilizer has finite jet quotient groups from which one can form induced representations. The note does not specify the topology on Diff, the representation category, the definition of functional dimension, the allowed jet order or an equivalence relation; it therefore does not determine an exhaustive classification theorem.',r'\citet[\S1.1, pp.~5--7]{vershikgelfandgraev1975diffeomorphisms} gives relevant examples of finite functional dimension and cites Kirillov\textquoteright s 1974 preprint. This is a thematic match, not a proof of the unqualified classification sentence.',[VGG],cls='F',literal='not determinable',match='thematic only',verification='read the result',confidence='medium',speaker='A. A. Kirillov',status='the construction has a documented later context, but the claimed universal classification is under-specified.')
save()

def fi(*args,**kw):
    kw.setdefault('subject','Algebraic K-theory and categorical topology')
    return item(311,*args,**kw)
talk(311,'1973-10-15','D. B. Fuchs','P000521-P000526','Quillen Theorem A, the Q construction and the plus comparison',r'The continuation starts below Kirillov on P000522 and ends on P000524, before the new date on P000525. It gives the dual comma category, Theorem A, exact categories, admissible base change, and Quillen\textquoteright s span category Q. It then compares the Q definition with the plus construction, explicitly retracting a guessed equality. The recorded plus formula has an erroneous one-degree shift. The direct finite-rank maps to BQ are legitimate but do not themselves supply the comparison to the loop space. \citet[\S\S1--2]{quillen1973higher} and \citet[IV.7]{weibel2013kbook} distinguish these points.')
fi('P000522','construction','The dual comma category and homotopy fibres',r'Alongside \(Y\backslash f\), the page defines \(f/Y\) with arrows \(f(X)\to Y\), and says the dual theorem holds.',r'For a functor f between small categories, the comma category f/Y has objects \((X,f(X)\to Y)\). A morphism \(Y\to Y^{\prime}\) induces \(f/Y\to f/Y^{\prime}\). If every such induced classifying-space map is a homotopy equivalence, the canonical map \(B(f/Y)\to\operatorname{hofib}_Y(Bf)\) is a homotopy equivalence. For \(Y\backslash f\) the variance is reversed.',r'\citet[Theorem~B and its dual, p.~97]{quillen1973higher}.',[Q],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction=r'Theorem B\textquoteright s arrow-invariance hypothesis remains necessary; a comma category is not automatically a homotopy fibre for every functor.')
fi('P000522','theorem','Quillen Theorem A',r'If every \(B(Y\backslash f)\) is contractible, \(Bf\) induces isomorphisms of homotopy groups.',r'If \(B(Y\backslash f)\) is contractible for every object Y of the small category \(C^{\prime}\), then \(Bf:BC\to BC^{\prime}\) is a homotopy equivalence. The dual criterion with f/Y is equally valid.',r'\citet[Theorem~A, p.~93]{quillen1973higher}.',[Q],reconstruction=r'Classifying spaces have CW type, so the weak-equivalence conclusion in the notes gives the stated homotopy equivalence, including the component condition.')
fi('P000522','definition','An exact category inside an abelian category',r'M is a full extension-closed subcategory of an abelian category A; monomorphisms and epimorphisms in its short exact sequences are admissible.',r'Let M be an essentially small, full additive subcategory of an abelian category A, closed under extensions. Give M the exact structure consisting of short exact sequences in A with all three terms in M. An admissible monomorphism has cokernel in M, and an admissible epimorphism has kernel in M.',r'\citet[\S2, pp.~99--100]{quillen1973higher}.',[Q],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction=r'Additivity and the requirement that every term belong to M are stated explicitly. Not every ambient monomorphism between objects of M is admissible.')
fi('P000522','theorem','Admissible epimorphisms survive pullback',r'For an admissible epimorphism \(Y\to X\) and arbitrary \(Z\to X\), the pullback lies in M and its arrow to Z is admissible.',r'If \(0\to K\to Y\to X\to0\) is exact in M and \(Z\to X\) is any M-morphism, the ambient pullback P satisfies \(0\to K\to P\to Z\to0\). Extension closure puts P in M and makes \(P\to Z\) an admissible epimorphism.',r'\citet[\S2, exact-category axiom (b), pp.~99--100]{quillen1973higher}.',[Q],reconstruction=r'This proof supplies exactly the closure needed for the following span composition.',development='annotated')
fi('P000523','construction','Morphisms in Q are admissible subquotient spans',r'Objects of QM equal those of M, and morphisms are \(X_1\leftarrow X\to X_2\), with admissible epi and mono, modulo the obvious equivalence.',r'A morphism \(X\to Y\) of QM is an isomorphism class of diagrams \(X\xleftarrow{p}E\xrightarrow{i}Y\), where p is admissible epic and i admissible monic. Isomorphisms of middle objects must commute with both arrows. Thus X is an admissible quotient of an admissible subobject of Y.',r'\citet[\S2, definition of Q, p.~101]{quillen1973higher}.',[Q],reconstruction=r'The equivalence relation is stated explicitly; reversing either admissibility label would define a different construction.')
fi('P000523','construction','Composition of Q spans by fibre product',r'The diagram defines \(X_4=X\times_{X_2}X^{\prime}\) and uses it as the middle object of the composite.',r'Compose \(X_1\leftarrow E\rightarrowtail X_2\) and \(X_2\twoheadleftarrow E^{\prime}\rightarrowtail X_3\) through \(E\times_{X_2}E^{\prime}\). The projection to E is admissible epic; the projection to \(E^{\prime}\) is admissible monic, being the kernel of the composite to the admissible cokernel of \(E\rightarrowtail X_2\). Associativity follows from canonical pullback isomorphisms.',r'\citet[\S2, definition of composition in Q, p.~101]{quillen1973higher}.',[Q],reconstruction=r'An admissible epi pullback stays in M by the preceding item; the kernel argument supplies the other admissibility. The corrected middle-object label X4 in the scan is respected.')
fi('P000523','definition','Higher K groups as shifted homotopy groups of BQ',r'\(K_i(M)=\pi_{i+1}(BQM,0)\); isomorphism classes of objects must form a set.',r'Choose a small skeleton of the exact category M and a zero object as basepoint. For \(i\ge0\), define \(K_i(M)=\pi_{i+1}(BQM,0)\). In degree zero this is canonically the Grothendieck group on objects with exact-sequence relations.',r'\citet[\S2, definition and Theorem~1, pp.~101--103]{quillen1973higher}.',[Q],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction=r'Essential smallness justifies the classifying-space construction; zero objects give equivalent choices of basepoint.')
fi('P000523','definition','The exact category of finitely generated projective modules',r'For an arbitrary ring A, PA consists of finitely generated projective left modules, and \(K_i(A)=K_i(PA)\).',r'For a unital ring A take P(A), a small skeleton of finitely generated projective unital left A-modules with its split exact structure. Its Q construction defines the connective algebraic K groups of A.',r'\citet[Introduction and \S2]{quillen1973higher}.',[Q],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction=r'The identity convention on rings and modules is explicit. All these short exact sequences split, but their span category and K groups are still nontrivial.')
fi('P000523','definition','The plus construction and its corrected degree',r'\(BGL(A)\to B_+GL(A)\) is an acyclic map, surjective on fundamental groups, with H-space target. The page then calls \(\pi_{i+1}(B_+GL(A))\) the old definition of Ki.',r'For stable \(GL(A)\), kill its perfect elementary subgroup by the plus construction. The map \(BGL(A)\to BGL(A)^+\) is acyclic, has fundamental group \(GL(A)/E(A)=K_1(A)\), and its target has an H-space structure. The correct convention is \(K_i(A)=\pi_i(BGL(A)^+)\) for \(i\ge1\). Degree zero is supplied separately by K0(A).',r'\citet[Introduction, pp.~85--86]{quillen1973higher}; precise conventions in \citet[Definition~IV.1.1 and IV.1.1.1]{weibel2013kbook}.',[Q,W],literal='false as written',reconstruction=r'The source transcription intentionally preserves i+1. That index is correct for BQ and incorrect for BGL-plus. The map and H-space requirements alone do not specify the plus construction without its perfect subgroup.')
fi('P000523','theorem','The plus/Q comparison needs a loop space',r'The guessed equality \(BQPA=B_+GL(A)\) is immediately followed by this is false, but there is a map.',r'The correct comparison is a homotopy equivalence \(\Omega BQP(A)\simeq K_0(A)\times BGL(A)^+\). Thus the identity component of the loop space corresponds to the connected plus space. This yields agreement of the two definitions in positive K degrees.',r'Announced in \citet[Introduction, p.~86]{quillen1973higher}; a full proof and exact statement are in \citet[Theorem~IV.7.1 and Corollary~IV.7.2]{weibel2013kbook}.',[Q,W],literal='false as written',reconstruction=r'The notebook itself retracts the unlooped equality. Both the loop and the discrete K0 factor matter; a mere map to BQ is insufficient to establish the comparison.',match='exact')
fi('P000523-P000524','construction','Finite-rank automorphisms give nullhomotopic maps to BQ',r'Viewing GL(n,A) as a one-object category, its action on \(S_n=A^n\) gives a functor to QP(A), hence \(BGL(n,A)\to BQP(A)\).',r'The stated functor exists: an automorphism g gives the span \(A^n\xleftarrow{\rm id}A^n\xrightarrow{g}A^n\). Its classifying-space map is nullhomotopic, because \(0\xleftarrow{\rm id}0\rightarrowtail A^n\) is invariant under these automorphisms and gives a natural transformation from the constant zero functor.',r'Functoriality of nerves and the natural-transformation homotopy are \citet[\S1, Proposition~2]{quillen1973higher}. The specific nullhomotopy is our direct check of the notebook construction, not a quoted plus/Q comparison theorem.',[Q],reconstruction=r'\ours{For every g the two composites of the displayed zero span agree, so its components form a natural transformation. Realizing its nerve gives the nullhomotopy. This works on the automorphism subcategory; it does not make the whole Q category contractible.}',development='annotated')
fi('P000524','construction','Adding a free summand is homotopic to the identity on BQ',r'\(X\mapsto X\oplus A\) is called a homotopy equivalence and used to align the finite-rank stabilization square.',r'Direct sum with A acts on a Q-span by summing A on each object and the identity on each arrow. The admissible inclusions \(X\to X\oplus A\), represented as Q-spans, are a natural transformation from the identity functor. Hence the induced map on BQP(A) is homotopic to the identity and is a homotopy equivalence. The finite-rank square commutes before realization with this right-hand functor.',r'\citet[\S1, Proposition~2]{quillen1973higher} supplies the homotopy mechanism. The direct check below establishes the exact stabilization sentence; the separate comparison theorem remains \citet[Corollary~IV.7.2]{weibel2013kbook}.',[Q,W],reconstruction=r'\ours{For a span \(X\xleftarrow{p}E\xrightarrow{i}Y\), pull back \(p\oplus1:E\oplus A\to X\oplus A\) along \(X\hookrightarrow X\oplus A\). The pullback is E, and both naturality composites are \(X\leftarrow E\to Y\oplus A\). Thus stabilization is homotopic to the identity. These unlooped maps do not by themselves identify the K-theory comparison.}',development='annotated')
save()
